material is different. 6. Also, boots don't roll, they experience sliding friction, but if the outsole is very thick (e.g., 20mm), it can take many years of walking. The original question was why shoes don't last nearly as long as tires, so we were comparing typical shoes (thin soles) to tires. Work boots with thick soles can indeed last a decade, but they're not typical shoes, and even then they're worn out in terms of distance much quicker than a tire. So the perception of longevity is decoupled from mileage. 7. Finally, tie it back to the core point: the engineering details (material compound, thickness, usage patterns) still explain everything, including dad's boots. I'll phrase it in a friendly, conversational way, addressing the "come on" tone.
00:02:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great counterexample—your dad’s work boots essentially *were* a pair of thick rubber slabs that held up for a decade, seemingly matching his truck tires. But this actually proves the engineering details rather than disproving them. Here’s why that case is the exception, not the rule: ### 1. Work boots aren’t typical “shoes” Those 10-year boots likely had: - A **solid rubber outsole** that was 15–25 mm thick (sometimes more), not the 3–5 mm found on sneakers or casual shoes. That’s comparable to a tire’s tread depth. - A **hard, high-density rubber compound** that was almost tire-like—often with carbon black, reinforced with fiber, and heavily vulcanized. They were designed for durability over comfort. - A **lug pattern** that lifted much of the sole off the ground, reducing the contact area and concentrating wear on the lugs, but those lugs were still deep enough to last years. So yes, when you make a shoe sole *as thick and tough as a tire tread*, it can last a very long time in terms of total calendar life. But that doesn’t mean the rubber wears at the same rate. ### 2. Time ≠ distance “Lasted ten years” sounds equal, but what was the mileage? - A pickup truck tire routinely goes **40,000–60,000 miles** in that time. - A pair of work boots worn every day for 10 years, walking an average of, say, 2–3 miles a day, would cover only about **7,000–10,000 miles** total. That’s roughly **1/5 to 1/10** the distance the tire traveled. Even if the boot’s outsole lost 10 mm of rubber in that 10,000 miles, the wear rate per mile would be about **1 mm per 1,000 miles**. A tire losing 8 mm over 50,000 miles wears at **0.16 mm per 1,000 miles**—roughly **6 times slower**. So the boot’s rubber is still wearing down much faster per mile; it’s just that there was so much of it that it lasted a decade at low annual mileage. ### 3. Operating conditions differ Work boots on a farm or job site might see: - Softer surfaces (dirt, gravel, mud) that are less abrasive than asphalt. - Less consistent, high-speed sliding. Farmers and tradesmen often walk with more of a heel-to-toe rolling motion on uneven ground, which reduces the scraping slide you get on pavement. - The thick lugged sole spreads pressure better, and the hard rubber resists cut and chunking, even if it’s still subject to abrasive wear. Truck tires, by contrast, run at highway speeds on hot asphalt with high cornering forces and frequent acceleration/braking cycles—but they’re engineered to handle that. ### 4. The “survivor” effect A pair of boots that truly lasted a decade was probably not worn every single day for 10 years in harsh factory grinding conditions. There may have been rotation, seasonal use, or the fact that once the lugs wore down close to the midsole, they were retired *before* full failure. A tire is legally retired when tread depth hits 2/32”, with many miles left before complete wear-through. Boots are often kept until the sole gets dangerously thin or the leather cracks, which can take many years if the outsole started out extremely thick. ### Bottom line Your dad’s boots and his truck tires **seemed** to last the same amount of time, but they traveled vastly different distances. The boots survived because of extreme thickness and hard rubber, not because the rubber wore at the same rate. A typical sneaker with a thin, soft sole would be dead in under 500 miles—same as a tire with 1/10 the tread depth would be gone in 5,000 miles. So you’re right that **thick rubber holds up** in the sense that if you pile on enough of it, even a moderately wear-resistant compound will take years to grind away at walking distances. But the *rubber itself* is not wearing at the same rate; the boot’s wear rate is still many times higher per mile. The engineering details—thickness, compound hardness, and total mileage accumulated—explain both the typical shoe and the outlier work boot perfectly.
