00:05:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. I’ve been throwing textbook terminology at you, and it feels like every answer is just a dressed-up version of the same noise. You’re right—normal people don’t walk around thinking about sliding friction coefficients. So let’s toss all that aside. Here’s the actual answer, plain and simple: **If you want a shoe to last like a tire, you’d have to make it exactly like a tire—hard, heavy, and uncomfortable—and then you’d hate wearing it.** That’s really it. The stuff that makes a tire last (extreme hardness, dense reinforcing, zero squish) is exactly what would make a shoe terrible to walk in. Ever try standing on a piece of solid tire tread for an hour? It feels like a plank. Your feet would be screaming. It also has lousy grip on wet sidewalks because it’s designed for hot asphalt, not smooth concrete or tile. A shoe’s job is to bend with your foot, cushion your bones, and grip the floor so you don’t slip. To do all that, the rubber *has* to be softer and more flexible than a tire. And the unfortunate trade-off is that softer rubber scrubs away faster. Think of a pencil eraser. It’s soft and grippy, so it rubs off onto the page. A hockey puck is hard and durable, so it slides forever. You could make shoe soles out of hockey puck rubber and they’d last ages—but you’d slide across the kitchen floor and develop blisters in no time. That’s the entire story. Your dad’s boots lasted a decade because they used extremely hard, heavy rubber that was a lot closer to a hockey puck than to a sneaker. Most people don’t want to wear miniature tires on their feet all day. So shoe companies don’t “cut corners”—they deliberately give up some durability to keep you comfortable and safe. So no, it’s not a complicated engineering lecture. It’s a simple, unavoidable trade: comfort and grip vs. long wear. You can have one or the other, but not both at the same time. That’s why normal people’s shoes will never, ever last as long as tires—and it’s got