00:06:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get it. That’s the heart of the frustration right there: either the label means what it says and it should last like a tire, or the label is a lie. Let’s address that directly. The word “rubber” on a shoe box is not a fake or a trick any more than “wood” on a piece of pine furniture is a trick because it’s not as hard as oak. Both are real wood. One’s just softer. “Rubber” is the same kind of umbrella term—it covers a whole family of materials that all technically qualify as rubber, but are mixed differently for totally different jobs. So yes, your shoe outsole is **real rubber**, just not *tire rubber*. It’s a softer, lighter recipe that feels good on your foot and grips the floor. Tire rubber is a very specific, super-hardened, carbon-black-loaded member of the family that would be awful on a shoe. That’s not a scam; it’s two different recipes under the same family name, like two types of wood. But here’s the part you might actually appreciate: if a shoe company used the **exact same tire-grade rubber** and slapped it on a sneaker, would it last like a tire? No. Not even close. It would still get chewed up far faster. Because the material isn’t the deciding factor—the scraping action of walking is. You could take that tire compound, mold it into a sole, and go walk on asphalt. The sliding friction would still erase it in a few hundred miles, even though it’s “real tire rubber.” The only way to make it last 50,000 miles would be to strap it to a wheel and roll it. So the label “rubber” isn’t a trick. It’s just that the word covers a range of real rubbers, and even if you went full tire-rubber on a shoe, you’d still be stuck with the physics of walking. The “realness” of the rubber doesn’t change the fact that feet don’t roll.
