00:14:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and that's a completely reasonable thing to feel. If it's true that the basic act of walking scrapes rubber away no matter what, then it sounds like we're just trapped in an endless, expensive cycle of buying shoes that can never truly last. That's disheartening. So let me give you the honest, non-smoothed-over answer about hope, because you deserve that. The truth is: yes, any shoe you actually wear will eventually wear out. The scraping action of walking on hard surfaces means no rubber sole will last forever. But “eventually” doesn’t have to mean “every six months.” There’s a big middle ground between a cheap sneaker that dies in 400 miles and the impossible fantasy of shoes lasting as long as tires. Here’s where the hope actually lives: - **Repairable shoes.** Old-school leather shoes with Goodyear welt or stitch-down construction can be resoled multiple times. The upper can last for decades, and you just replace the rubber when it grinds down. That’s not “buying new shoes forever”; it’s maintaining one good pair. This still exists today. Brands like Red Wing, Solovair, and many classic dress shoe makers still build shoes this way. They cost more upfront, but they’re not disposable. - **Harder rubber compounds.** Some modern shoe companies already use high-abrasion rubber in the heel and forefoot. They’re not tire-hard, but they’re a step up. You can seek those out. They won’t last 50,000 miles, but they can last noticeably longer than the typical soft foam-and-rubber hybrid. - **Rotation.** Having two pairs of daily shoes and alternating them can more than double the combined lifespan, because the materials get time to dry out and recover between wears. This isn't a gimmick; it genuinely slows wear. - **Surfaces matter.** Walking on grass, dirt, or rubberized tracks wears shoes far slower than asphalt and concrete. If you’re mostly on pavement, that’s the most abrasive environment. There’s no fix for that, but it’s worth knowing. So you’re not completely stuck. The problem isn’t that durable shoes are impossible; it’s that the most common, affordable shoes today are designed for comfort and lightness first, with durability a distant third. That’s a market choice, not a law of nature. You can opt out by buying resoleable, harder-soled footwear and taking care of them. And yes, they will still eventually wear down—but on a timeline measured in years or even a decade, not months. That’s not tire-like, but it’s also not hopeless. I’m sorry the earlier part of the conversation made it sound like there’s no escape. There is. It just requires knowing what to look for. If you ever want practical pointers on finding that kind of shoe, I’m happy to help. No lectures, just straight talk.
